# 10. SIMULTANEOUS MULTI-VAN SALES

This is one of the most important acceptance requirements.

Consider two salespeople operating concurrently.

### Scenario A — Different vans

Van 001 has 20 units of Product A.

Van 002 has 15 units of Product A.

Salesperson A sells five units from Van 001.

Salesperson B sells seven units from Van 002.

Expected result:

```text
Van 001: 20 - 5 = 15
Van 002: 15 - 7 = 8
```

Both sales commit independently with unique invoice IDs and invoice numbers.

Neither sale corrupts the other's stock.

### Scenario B — Same van

Two authorized employees attempt to sell the last available unit of a product from the same van at approximately the same time.

The backend must serialize or otherwise safely resolve the competing stock mutations.

Only one transaction may commit against that single available unit.

The other must receive a clear insufficient-stock or conflict outcome.

### Scenario C — Network interruption

A salesperson begins a sale offline.

The app stores the provisional operation in its durable local outbox.

When connectivity returns, the operation is submitted with its original idempotency key.

If already committed, the server returns the existing transaction outcome.

If there is a stock or authorization conflict, the app must display it for resolution.

The app must never silently create a duplicate invoice.

